<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muriq Shpata</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1415">1415</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muriq Shpata was an Albanian ruler of the Despotate of Arta in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, succeeding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gjin-bua-shpata">Gjin Bua Shpata</a> in 1399. His family had taken Arta during the Albanian expansion into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> in the 14th century. He defended the city against the Tocco rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ioannina">Ioannina</a> and, like his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaqub-shpata">Yaqub Shpata</a>, sought Ottoman support before Arta fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlo-i-tocco">Carlo I Tocco</a> in 1416.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Muriq, also known as Maurice Shpata, was a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gjin-bua-shpata">Gjin Bua Shpata</a>, the first Albanian ruler of Arta, and was born there in the 14th century. He had a full brother, Yaqub, and two half-siblings, Charles Marchesano and Maddalena, from his mother's second marriage. Shortly before his death on 29 October 1399, Gjin named his own brother Skurra Bua Shpata, ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nafpaktos">Naupactus</a>, as his successor, but within days the adventurer Vonko seized Arta. Muriq drove Vonko out, possibly as early as December 1399, and took over the city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Much of his rule was taken up by war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlo-i-tocco">Carlo I Tocco</a>. In 1402–03 he helped Skurra repel Tocco's general Galasso Peccatore at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angelokastron">Angelokastron</a>, and in 1406, when Tocco and the rival Bua brothers ravaged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acarnania">Acarnania</a> and the country round Arta, he held the city. He refused to help his cousin Pal Shpata, who then ceded Angelokastron to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and sold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nafpaktos">Naupactus</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in 1407–08. Around 1410 Muriq allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esau-de-buondelmonti">Esau de' Buondelmonti</a>, Despot of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ioannina">Ioannina</a>, who had married and then divorced his mother Irene, and sealed the pact by marrying a daughter to Esau's son Giorgio.</p><p>Esau's death on 6 February 1411 opened a contest for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ioannina">Ioannina</a>. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gjon-zenebishi">Gjon Zenebishi</a>, lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gjirokaster">Gjirokastër</a>, Muriq besieged the city twice in vain, and its people, opposed to an Albanian or Serbian ruler, surrendered it to Tocco. In spring or summer 1412 the two Albanian lords almost destroyed Tocco's army at Kranea, in the district of Mesopotamon, but still could not take Ioannina. Tocco then allied with the Ottoman prince Musa Çelebi, while Muriq turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/centurione-ii-zaccaria">Centurione II Zaccaria</a>, Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a>. A scandal involving Zenebishi's son and a son-in-law of Muriq broke the alliance, and Zenebishi made peace with Tocco.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On his death Muriq was succeeded in Arta by his brother Yaqub, while Charles Marchesano received the governorship of Rogoi. His successors could not hold off Tocco: in October 1416 Yaqub was captured and killed in an ambush, and Arta surrendered, ending Albanian rule in the area. From his marriage to Nerata, a Serbian woman, Muriq had daughters, usually counted as three, who married Giorgio de' Buondelmonti, Simon Zenebishi and, after their father's death, Carlo II Tocco.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Maurice Spata” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Losha's death in 1374 Gjin Bua Shpata ruled both despotates until 1399, followed by <b>Muriq Shpata</b> and Yaqub Shpata, who allied with the Ottomans.</blockquote><a href="/state/despotate-of-arta">Despotate of Arta</a></li></ul></section>
